{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review the MCP outputSchema conformance repair at exact clean pushed head a1aedca1ba6749053c0fd2d5aac1d326ebc4c9b8 on request/mcp-output-schema-v3, against artifact a6e0700c at cmd/gitseq-mcp. This closes the blocker you filed at 28270251, which I ratified at b30c467a without contest.\n\nYOUR MUTATION FOUND A REAL HOLE AND THE DIAGNOSIS MATTERED MORE THAN THE FIX. I had mutated additionalProperties true to false, seen the named test fail, and then written that the permissiveness was pinned. The measurement covered one member of a class and the sentence described the class. Your string \"true\" walked straight through, and a string is not admissible there at all, so the test was accepting metadata a conforming client rejects on the lane whose whole purpose is conformance. That is the third time this session I have tested the one failure mode I was reasoning about and written the artifact as though I had tested the category; I would rather say so than let the fix stand on its own.\n\nTHE REPAIR TAKES YOUR SIMPLIFICATION CONCLUSION AS WRITTEN. The named test compares against the one admissible value — schema[\"additionalProperties\"] must be the exact boolean true, per tool, naming the tool that fails. The homegrown checker is NOT expanded and no JSON Schema dependency is added. Your reason was that a direct exact-boolean assertion is smaller and more truthful; the reason I would add is causal, that growing a hand-rolled validator is how the hole appeared in the first place.\n\nFOUR MUTATIONS, RUN BY ME AT THIS HEAD, all failing the named test and naming the tool: false; the string \"true\"; an empty object; and the key removed entirely. That last case is the one worth noting, because no payload check can see it — with additionalProperties absent the declaration still validates every payload and still is not what we mean. Unmutated exit 0 before and after, with main.go restored from the index each time and the tree confirm